# Copy this file to .env in the repo root and fill in only what you use.
# The app loads .env automatically at startup (API, CLI, and demo).
# A variable set in the real environment always takes precedence over .env.
#
# THE STANDARD RUN IS A REAL PROVIDER API — that is how this agent is
# meant to run, and how enterprises run agents: set MODEL_BACKEND to
# anthropic or openai below and add your key. (Any OpenAI-compatible
# endpoint works via OPENAI_BASE_URL.)
# The mock backend exists only as a no-key smoke test of the plumbing;
# Ollama (below) is the local/sovereign alternative.

# Which model backend to use: anthropic | openai | ollama | mock
MODEL_BACKEND=anthropic

# Which retriever to use: keyword | semantic | hybrid
# RETRIEVER=keyword

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenAI (drafting with MODEL_BACKEND=openai; also the embeddings provider
# for RETRIEVER=semantic|hybrid — Anthropic has no embeddings API)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini
# Optional: any OpenAI-compatible endpoint — Azure OpenAI, a LiteLLM
# gateway, Together, Groq, or another hosted/self-hosted compatible
# service. This one variable is the "other providers" switch.
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://your-endpoint.example/v1
# Embedding model for RETRIEVER=semantic|hybrid.
# OPENAI_EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Anthropic (drafting with MODEL_BACKEND=anthropic)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-5
# Optional: a custom Anthropic-compatible endpoint.
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://your-endpoint.example

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NVIDIA NIM — the endpoints NVIDIA's own blueprints deploy on.
# Hosted API catalog (build.nvidia.com): OpenAI-compatible, use
# MODEL_BACKEND=openai with your NVIDIA API key:
#   OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1
#   OPENAI_MODEL=nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b   (or another NIM model)
#   OPENAI_API_KEY=nvapi-...
# (model name verified live against the hosted catalog 2026-10-10 —
# the full demo ran on it end to end; hosted catalog names rotate, so
# if a name returns 410/404, pick its successor on build.nvidia.com)
# Self-hosted NIM container (enterprise deployment, GPU): same settings
# with OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000/v1 and the model your
# container serves.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ollama — fully local real-LLM mode (MODEL_BACKEND=ollama).
# No API key: Ollama serves an OpenAI-compatible API on localhost.
# Setup: install Ollama, then `ollama pull llama3.1` (and
# `ollama pull nomic-embed-text` if you use RETRIEVER=semantic|hybrid —
# embeddings come from Ollama too in this mode).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OLLAMA_MODEL=llama3.1
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
# OLLAMA_EMBEDDING_MODEL=nomic-embed-text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared / optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Request timeout (seconds) for provider API calls. Provider SDK retries
# are disabled in code, so a dead endpoint fails within this timeout
# instead of stalling on silent retries.
# LLM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
# Resilience ceiling (seconds) per provider call at a pipeline node —
# the wrapper in resilience.py, above the per-request timeout above.
# A call past the ceiling becomes a clean provider error; the
# idempotent language steps (extract, classify, diagnose, options,
# verify, review) retry once. Drafting never retries.
# NODE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=180
# Agentic diagnosis (provider mode): the diagnosis runs a bounded tool
# loop — the model may call search_policies / lane_history /
# shipment_facts / carrier_history before composing. This caps the tool
# calls per diagnosis (default 4, hard cap 6).
# DIAGNOSIS_MAX_TOOL_CALLS=4
# Bounded repair loop: when a draft fails the guardrails, redraft with
# the failure reasons fed back, then re-validate. On by default; set to
# off/0/false/no to disable. Max redraft attempts per run (hard cap 3).
# GUARDRAIL_REPAIR=on
# GUARDRAIL_REPAIR_MAX_ATTEMPTS=1
# Independent reviewer: a separate model call (or a second, different
# checklist in mock mode) reviews the draft after self-verification —
# grounding, policy compliance, tone, packet completeness. On by
# default; REVIEWER=off disables the step. REVIEWER_MODEL points the
# review at a different model than the drafter (default: the run's).
# REVIEWER=on
# REVIEWER_MODEL=
# Per-run token budget (cost guardrail): once one run's provider tokens
# pass this cap, the remaining provider steps degrade to their
# deterministic paths — the run finishes, the trace notes the degraded
# steps, and telemetry reports {limit, used, exceeded}. Unset = off.
# RUN_TOKEN_BUDGET=
# Output routing (off by default): when set, a successful approval POSTs
# the approved packet JSON to this URL — your TMS / ticket queue /
# automation endpoint. Failures are recorded on the result as
# dispatch_status=failed and never undo the approval.
# ACTION_WEBHOOK_URL=
# ACTION_WEBHOOK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5
# Delivery bookkeeping: every attempt is recorded on the record's
# delivery ledger (GET /shipments/{id}/dispatch). A failed delivery
# can be retried (POST /shipments/{id}/dispatch/retry) up to this
# many TOTAL attempts, with exponential backoff between them
# (base seconds, doubling per attempt).
# ACTION_WEBHOOK_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
# ACTION_WEBHOOK_RETRY_BASE_SECONDS=30
# Shared secret for signing webhook deliveries: when set, each POST
# carries X-Trida-Signature: sha256=<HMAC-SHA256 hex of the body> so
# the receiving system can verify the packet really came from the
# agent (and was not altered) before acting on it.
# ACTION_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Model for the opt-in LLM-judge eval pack (evals/run_llm_evals.py);
# defaults to the backend's own model.
# LLM_JUDGE_MODEL=
# Approval-queue SLA budgets: how many hours a case of each severity
# may await a human decision before the queue flags it sla_breach
# (GET /queue + the console). A wait past budget is itself treated
# as an exception worth a human's attention.
# QUEUE_SLA_HOURS_CRITICAL=4
# QUEUE_SLA_HOURS_HIGH=24
# QUEUE_SLA_HOURS_MEDIUM=48
# QUEUE_SLA_HOURS_LOW=96
# The escalation ladder's second rung, as a multiple of the budget:
# a breach reported while still under FACTOR x its budget that keeps
# aging past that threshold re-fires as a signed sla_escalation
# webhook event carrying the wait duration (same opt-in channel as
# the breach event below; ledgered and deduped per rung). The factor
# is per-severity tunable: QUEUE_SLA_ESCALATION_FACTOR_<SEVERITY>
# overrides the global value for that severity (a critical case can
# escalate at 1.5x while a low one waits for 3x); every value clamps
# to at least 1 — never before the breach it escalates.
# QUEUE_SLA_ESCALATION_FACTOR=2
# QUEUE_SLA_ESCALATION_FACTOR_CRITICAL=1.5
# QUEUE_SLA_ESCALATION_FACTOR_LOW=3
# Worker staleness: how old a background worker's last recorded
# sweep may get before the deployment calls it stale. Per-worker
# (WORKER_STALE_SECONDS_<WORKER>) over the global value; unset =
# the worker is not watched. A stale watched worker flags on
# /readiness and /metrics, and the SLA sweep fires one signed
# worker_stale event per staleness episode on the SLA channel.
# WORKER_STALE_SECONDS=900
# WORKER_STALE_SECONDS_DISPATCH_RETRIES=300
# SLA breach events (off by default): when 'on', the sweep
# (shipment-agent sla-sweep, or POST /queue/sla-sweep) fires ONE
# signed sla_breach webhook event the first time it observes a
# queued shipment past its SLA budget — deduped per record, and
# ledgered on the record's own SLA ledger. Delivery goes to
# SLA_BREACH_WEBHOOK_URL when set, else to ACTION_WEBHOOK_URL, and
# is signed with ACTION_WEBHOOK_SECRET when that is set.
# SLA_BREACH_WEBHOOK=on
# SLA_BREACH_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Multi-tenancy: every record belongs to a tenant and every read is
# scoped to one tenant's partition (the store's identity is the pair
# tenant_id + shipment_id; migration 0004). Requests name their
# tenant with the X-Tenant-ID header; this variable is the tenant a
# process serves when a request carries no header. Unset = the
# 'default' tenant (the single-tenant shape).
# TENANT_ID=
# Log verbosity for the API's structured JSON logs (observability.py).
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO
# OpenTelemetry trace export (tracing.py): when the endpoint is set
# (and the 'otel' extra is installed), every graph run exports a span
# per node and per provider call — attributes limited to ids and
# counts, never shipment content. Unset = off (the default posture).
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318
# OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=shipment-agent

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Persistence — PostgreSQL is the system of record (production shape)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One database holds the approval store, the LangGraph checkpoints,
# and the pgvector policy embeddings. The compose stack sets this
# for you; a managed Postgres (RDS, Cloud SQL, Azure Database) works
# the same — it must have the pgvector extension available.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://logistics:logistics@localhost:5432/logistics
# Schema lives in migrations/ (numbered SQL) and is applied at
# startup (API lifespan; the Postgres collaborators also ensure it
# on construction). The LangGraph checkpointer's own tables are
# created by the official Postgres saver's setup().
# Without DATABASE_URL the service runs on in-memory TEST DOUBLES:
# nothing persists. That mode exists for tests and offline smoke
# runs — there is deliberately no local-file runtime option. The
# SQLite store/checkpointer survive as test doubles, selectable for
# the persistence tests and throwaway local runs:
# STATE_DB_PATH=            # SQLite test-double store (path or :memory:)
# CHECKPOINT_DB_PATH=       # SQLite test-double checkpointer location
# Checkpointed approval gate: with checkpoints on (default), a run
# pauses in the graph at the approval gate and approve/reject resumes
# its thread (thread_id = the shipment id). The store above stays the
# record of decisions; the checkpointer holds graph state only.
# CHECKPOINTS=on

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Object storage — shipment documents (S3-compatible)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# When S3_BUCKET is set, shipment documents are objects: adapters
# may submit just an object key (the service fetches the text), and
# inline documents are archived under shipments/<id>/documents/
# with their keys recorded on the stored shipment. Credentials come
# from the standard AWS chain (AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
# or the runtime's role). The compose stack points this at MinIO.
# S3_BUCKET=shipment-documents
# S3_ENDPOINT_URL=http://localhost:9000   # MinIO / custom endpoint; omit for AWS S3
# S3_REGION=us-east-1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vector store alternative — Chroma server
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# With DATABASE_URL set, semantic/hybrid retrieval uses pgvector in
# that database (the production path). A Chroma SERVER is a supported
# alternative behind the retriever interface when pgvector is not in
# play; it needs the vectordb extra. (An embedded CHROMA_DIR store
# still works for legacy tests but is not a runtime option we
# document — local vector files are not the production story.)
# CHROMA_HOST=            # Chroma server host
# CHROMA_PORT=8000
# CHROMA_DIR=.chroma      # embedded store location (legacy/tests)
# API key for the FastAPI app. When set, read + mutating endpoints
# require the X-API-Key header. When unset, the API is open (local dev).
# API_KEY=
# Per-tenant API keys: bind a tenant partition to a credential, so a
# key opens only its own tenant. Configure either as one variable of
# comma-separated tenant:key pairs, or one variable per tenant
# (API_KEY_<TENANT>, tenant id uppercased, non-alphanumerics folded
# to _). As soon as ANY per-tenant key is configured, the API is in
# the per-tenant model: a tenant's key is required for that tenant,
# a real key presented for the wrong tenant is a 403 (a missing or
# unknown key is a 401), and the shared API_KEY above keeps working
# for the 'default' tenant only. With NO per-tenant keys configured,
# the shared-key model applies and X-Tenant-ID is a trusted claim —
# fine for a single-client deployment on a trusted network, and the
# docs say so plainly.
# TENANT_API_KEYS=acme:key-one,globex:key-two
# API_KEY_ACME=key-one
# Key rotation: a tenant's OUTGOING key, accepted beside the current
# one until a grace deadline — so rotating a key is not a hard
# cutover that 401s the tenant's in-flight callers. Configure the
# previous key the same two ways (pairs, or API_KEY_PREVIOUS_<TENANT>),
# record when the rotation happened (pairs, or
# TENANT_KEY_ROTATED_AT_<TENANT>), and bound the window in hours
# (per-tenant TENANT_KEY_GRACE_HOURS_<TENANT> wins; default 72).
# Without a rotation timestamp the previous key opens no window.
# Every analysis records which key generation authenticated it (an
# id like acme:previous — never the secret) in the audit export;
# GET /auth/rotation and /metrics show the old key's remaining use.
# TENANT_PREVIOUS_API_KEYS=acme:old-key
# API_KEY_PREVIOUS_ACME=old-key
# TENANT_KEY_ROTATED_AT=acme:2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00
# TENANT_KEY_GRACE_HOURS=72
# Completion assist: how long the previous key must record no use
# before its window is reported READY TO CLOSE (ready_to_close on
# GET /auth/rotation, shipment_agent_tenant_key_rotation_closing in
# /metrics, and the escalation digest's open-windows section).
# Per-tenant TENANT_KEY_QUIET_HOURS_<TENANT> wins; default 24.
# Readiness never retires the key — unsetting it stays your change.
# TENANT_KEY_QUIET_HOURS=24
